ion":"If $A=\\{1,2\\}$ and $B=\\{3,4\\}$ in $S=\\{1,2,3,4\\}$, are $A$ and $B$ disjoint?","options":["No, because $A\\cup B=S$","Yes, because $A\\cap B=\\varnothing$","No, because both sets have two elements","Yes, because $A=B$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"6056e630732ae1a6cce7a950","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"pt_batch2_disjoint_events_009","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"If $A=\\{1,2,3\\}$ and $B=\\{3,4\\}$, are $A$ and $B$ disjoint?","options":["No, because they share the outcome $3$","Yes, because neither set is empty","Yes, because their union has four outcomes","No, because disjoint events must have equal size"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"06bb5911132e530ca2db480e","time_limit":25,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"pt_batch2_disjoint_events_011","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why does the addition formula simplify for disjoint events?","options":["The events must be independent","The union of the events is always the sample space","There is no overlap $A\\cap B$ to subtract","The probabilities are always equal"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ec5a4666eef2f3df2fdc2243","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"pt_batch2_disjoint_events_012","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"If one disjoint event occurs, what can be said about the other?","options":["It must also occur","It is ruled out for that trial","It becomes the complement of the sample space","It gets probability one"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"91e3a3f4df7edd55ccae9fd6","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"pt_batch2_disjoint_events_014","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"On one die roll, let $A$ be 'even' and $B$ be 'greater than $4$'. Are they disjoint?","options":["Yes, because $A$ has three outcomes","No, because outcome $6$ belongs to both","Yes, because $B$ has two outcomes","No, because every die outcome belongs to both"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"7a71adf30faf79031bcf18be","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"pt_batch2_disjoint_events_015","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"On one die roll, let $A$ be 'less than $3$' and $B$ be 'greater than $4$'. What is $A\\cap B$?","options":["$\\{1,2,5,6\\}$","$\\{3,4\\}$","$\\varnothing$","$\\{2,5\\}$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"5e4636d394b38ec4ba7ef41c","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"no_diagnostic_value"},{"id":"pt_batch2_disjoint_events_016","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Passing and failing the same exam attempt are best described as what?","options":["Independent events with positive probabilities","Disjoint events","The same event","Events whose intersection is the whole sample space"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"396722839edf3f4fd0420227","time_limit":30,"curation_status":"retired","retired_at":"2026-10-27T08:58:47.943104+00:00","reason_code":"duplicate_diagnostic_target"},{"id":"pt_batch2_disjoint_events_017","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why are two positive-probability disjoint events not independent?","options":["Independence would require $P(A\\cap B)=P(A)P(B)>0$, but disjointness gives $P(A\\cap B)=0$","Independence would require $A\\cup B=\\varnothing$","Disjointness makes both proba